# DKMS installation and recovery

Choose DKMS to rebuild the kernel module automatically when kernels change.
DKMS owns only `crystalhd.ko`; `make install-runtime` installs the firmware,
device permissions, library and frontends. For the simpler current-kernel
installation, use the [direct route](BRINGUP.md#direct-install-update-and-rollback).
Do not run `make install` over a DKMS-managed module.

## Prepare the source

Install the [Quick start dependencies](README.md#quick-start), plus DKMS:

```sh
sudo apt install dkms "linux-headers-$(uname -r)"
```

Work from the root of the source version you want to install. A source archive
works without `.git`. Keep the previous version's source/archive and any local
CrystalHD `/etc/modprobe.d/` option files for rollback. Retain the previous
revision's cached DKMS build for the running kernel as well. Read the DKMS
revision from this source. The `1.1.0` source uses
module/DKMS revision `3.10.9`; published `1.1.0-rc.5` retains `3.10.8`,
`1.1.0-rc.4` retains `3.10.7`,
`1.1.0-rc.3` retains `3.10.6` and the
earlier frozen, unpublished `1.1.0-rc.2` uses `3.10.5`. Preserve previous
registered source/builds for rollback.
Stable `1.0.0` uses `3.10.3`, and the published
`v1.0.0-rc.1` retains `3.10.2`. A checkout identity does not mean an RC was
published. Neither a retained source nor an existing registered source tree
should be overwritten:

```sh
kernel_release=$(uname -r)
dkms_version=$(sed -n 's/^PACKAGE_VERSION="\(.*\)"/\1/p' dkms.conf)
test -n "$dkms_version"
dkms_source="/usr/src/crystalhd-$dkms_version"
dkms status -m crystalhd
ls -ld -- "$dkms_source"
./scripts/crystalhd-check
```

A missing source path is expected on first registration. If `dkms_version` is
empty, stop and check the source directory. If the path already exists, use
[update](#update-an-existing-installation); do not overwrite it. If the check
finds a direct module, follow [switch from a direct install](#switch-from-a-direct-install).

## Install a new revision

Continue only when the revision's source path is absent and all playback
clients are closed; check the [idle preconditions](BRINGUP.md#reload-an-idle-device)
before installing files. Keep a separate source copy so later edits to your
working checkout cannot change future DKMS rebuilds. Paste complete
parenthesized blocks: they stop at a failed command without closing your
terminal. Build userspace before changing the installed module:

```sh
(
    set -eu
    kernel_release=$(uname -r)
    dkms_version=$(sed -n 's/^PACKAGE_VERSION="\(.*\)"/\1/p' dkms.conf)
    test -n "$dkms_version"
    dkms_source="/usr/src/crystalhd-$dkms_version"
    if test -e "$dkms_source" || test -L "$dkms_source"; then
        printf '%s\n' 'STOP: this revision already has a source path; use the update instructions.' >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    make -j2 library gstreamer vaapi
    sudo cp -a -- "$PWD" "$dkms_source"
    sudo dkms add -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version"
    sudo dkms build -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" -k "$kernel_release"
)
```

Only after that block succeeds, install the built module and matching runtime.
If switching from a direct module, complete the removal step in
[switch from a direct install](#switch-from-a-direct-install) first:

```sh
(
    set -eu
    kernel_release=$(uname -r)
    dkms_version=$(sed -n 's/^PACKAGE_VERSION="\(.*\)"/\1/p' dkms.conf)
    test -n "$dkms_version"
    sudo dkms install -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" -k "$kernel_release"
    sudo make install-runtime
)
```

Stop at any failed command. Keep `/usr/src/crystalhd-$dkms_version` available
for future kernel builds, including when you started from a downloaded archive.
Use `make -j1 library gstreamer vaapi` if a parallel userspace build exhausts
memory. DKMS build failures are reported in its build `make.log`; the source
build itself does not open the card.

For a first install, run `sudo modprobe crystalhd`. For an update, use the
[idle reload procedure](BRINGUP.md#reload-an-idle-device), or reboot. Then run:

```sh
dkms status -m crystalhd -k "$kernel_release"
crystalhd-check
```

Expect the chosen revision to be `installed` for this kernel and no
loaded/installed mismatch. Continue with [first hardware playback](USAGE.md).
If module loading reports a key/signature error, follow your distribution's
Secure Boot module-signing procedure; a successful DKMS build alone does not
authorize a module to load.

## Switch from a direct install

Inspect `crystalhd-check` and `modinfo -F filename crystalhd` before removal.
Only if they identify this project's direct file
`/lib/modules/$kernel_release/updates/crystalhd.ko`, close playback clients and
meet the idle preconditions. Complete source preparation and the first
[new-revision block](#install-a-new-revision) through the successful userspace
and DKMS builds before removing anything. Keep the old direct source for
rollback. From the source root, remove the direct file:

```sh
sudo make uninstall-module KVER="$kernel_release"
```

This removes only the configured direct module, preserves runtime files and
`updates/dkms/crystalhd.ko*`, and does not unload a running module. Stop if
removal fails. Continue with the second new-revision block to install the
already-built DKMS module and runtime, then reload or reboot. If installation
fails, remove the new DKMS registration and reinstall the previous direct
module from its retained source before rebooting. Other paths, compressed
direct modules or distribution-owned packages need their own removal method;
do not delete arbitrary candidates listed by the check.

## Update an existing installation

For a new DKMS revision, follow [prepare](#prepare-the-source) and [install](#install-a-new-revision)
from the new source. Keep the old revision/source until the new playback test
passes. The `1.1.0` source uses module/DKMS revision `3.10.9`,
upgrading RC.5's `3.10.8`, RC.4's `3.10.7` or stable `3.10.3` without changing the
`libcrystalhd.so.3` ABI. Use `dkms.conf` from the chosen source; a corrected RC
payload must use a new revision rather than replacing a registered source
tree. In particular, keep `/usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.8` unchanged when adding
`/usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.9`.

If reinstalling the **same revision from unchanged source**, inspect its
registered location first:

```sh
readlink -f -- "$dkms_source"
dkms status -m crystalhd
```

For a legacy symlink, confirm its target is the intended retained source tree.
Do not point the same revision at unrelated or edited sources. Release builds
use a distinct revision when packaged code changes. With clients closed and
the device idle, rebuild the selected kernel explicitly:

```sh
(
    set -eu
    : "${dkms_version:?Complete source preparation first}"
    : "${kernel_release:?Complete source preparation first}"
    make -j2 library gstreamer vaapi
    sudo dkms build --force -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" -k "$kernel_release"
    sudo dkms install --force -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" -k "$kernel_release"
    sudo make install-runtime
)
```

Run the runtime commands from the matching user-owned source tree. `dkms --force`
replaces a recorded build of the same revision; it is **not forced module
unloading**. Use ordinary idle reload or reboot, then check identity and play a
file. Do not use `rmmod -f` or a force-unload option.

## Roll back

Close players and satisfy the [idle preconditions](BRINGUP.md#reload-an-idle-device).
Inspect `dkms status` and select the previous revision you retained. Confirm its
cached module belongs to the known working installation and its writable
source/archive is the matching snapshot for userspace. A formerly mutable
`/usr/src` symlink or a matching version label alone does not prove identity.

Run the following from the outgoing version's source root. Replace both
previous-version values with real entries; `3.10.0` is a historical example.
The block requires the cached old module for this kernel/architecture and
finishes the userspace build before uninstalling anything:

```sh
(
    set -eu
    previous_revision="3.10.0"
    previous_source="/path/to/retained-previous-source"
    kernel_release=$(uname -r)
    kernel_arch=$(uname -m)
    dkms_version=$(sed -n 's/^PACKAGE_VERSION="\(.*\)"/\1/p' dkms.conf)
    test -n "$dkms_version"
    test "$previous_revision" != "$dkms_version"
    test -f "$previous_source/Makefile" || {
        printf '%s\n' 'STOP: select the matching retained previous source.' >&2
        exit 1
    }
    test "$(sed -n 's/^PACKAGE_VERSION="\(.*\)"/\1/p' "$previous_source/dkms.conf")" = "$previous_revision"
    previous_status=$(dkms status -m crystalhd -v "$previous_revision" -k "$kernel_release" -a "$kernel_arch")
    case "$previous_status" in
        *": built"|*": installed"*) ;;
        *) printf '%s\n' 'STOP: no retained DKMS build; preserve the current installation.' >&2; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    make -C "$previous_source" -j2 library gstreamer vaapi
    sudo dkms uninstall -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" -k "$kernel_release" -a "$kernel_arch"
    sudo dkms install -m crystalhd -v "$previous_revision" -k "$kernel_release" -a "$kernel_arch"
    sudo make uninstall-runtime
    sudo make -C "$previous_source" install-runtime
)
```

If the cached build is missing, stop and recover a verified saved DKMS build
before using this rollback route. Do not rebuild the old module from a mutable
source path or use `dkms build --force`: that would replace the saved binary.
The outgoing source's `uninstall-runtime`
removes release metadata/helpers that an older install might leave behind.
Stop on failure. Restore saved module-option files before reload or
reboot, removing options absent from the older module. Run `crystalhd-check`
and use the old version's playback instructions; a Phase 1 rollback removes
later optional features. After successful rollback, use `dkms status` to
identify the rejected revision and remove its registration with
`sudo dkms remove -m crystalhd -v "REJECTED_REVISION" --all`, replacing the
quoted placeholder, so it cannot be selected for future kernels. Keep its
source until the problem has been diagnosed.

## Uninstall

Inspect `dkms status -m crystalhd` and confirm which revision you intend to
remove. Close players and satisfy the idle preconditions first. From that
revision's source root, use the `dkms_version` read in [prepare](#prepare-the-source):

```sh
(
    set -eu
    : "${dkms_version:?Complete source preparation first}"
    if test -d /sys/module/crystalhd; then
        test "$(cat /sys/module/crystalhd/refcnt)" = 0
        sudo modprobe -r crystalhd
    fi
    sudo dkms remove -m crystalhd -v "$dkms_version" --all
)
```

If the module is already absent, skip its unload. Stop if normal unloading
fails; reboot and retry before playback. Repeat the DKMS removal for any other
revision you deliberately installed. Only after deciding to remove the whole
playback stack, with no other CrystalHD install using its files:

```sh
sudo make uninstall-runtime
```

This removes firmware, the udev rule, library, headers and frontends; it does
not remove browser profiles or local option files. Remove any saved CrystalHD
options you added if abandoning the installation. Keep source directories for
rollback; DKMS removal does not require deleting them. Check `dkms status` and
`modinfo -F filename crystalhd` afterward for an unexpected remaining module.
